  "source_only_primary_extract_utf8": "REGION algorithm-and-prop31 [250262, 284344]\nThe direct harmonic generator (3.3)\nstill bounces against the full gradient $\\nabla V$, so its bounce rate\ncan remain large.\nTo further reduce the bounce rate and the resulting computational\ncost, we introduce a conditional reference point and use the smoothness of\n$V$ to control the local gradient difference. Specifically, draw\n$\\widetilde{X}\\sim\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}$ independently and write the exact\ndecomposition\n$\\nabla V(x)=\\nabla V(\\widetilde{X})+\\{\\nabla V(x)-\\nabla V(\\widetilde{X})\\}\\,.$\nConditionally on $\\widetilde{X}=\\widetilde{x}$, we incorporate the fixed\nreference term into the deterministic dynamics and retain only the gradient\ndifference in the BPS jump term. Define the equilibrium position of this\ndeterministic harmonic flow and the residual gradient by\n$c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\coloneqq y-\\eta\\nabla V(\\widetilde{x})\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pth_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)\\coloneqq\\nabla V(x)-\\nabla V(\\widetilde{x})\\,.$\n(3.4)\nThe resulting generator is\n$\\begin{split}\\mathsf{L}_{y,\\widetilde{x}}f(x,p)&\\coloneqq\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p^{\\top}\\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\\frac{(x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{\\eta}}\\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,[p^{\\top}h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\\bigl\\{f(x,R_{h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\\bigr\\}\\,.\\end{split}$\n(3.5)\nThe harmonic and residual terms sum to the full conditional force\n$\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,\\nabla V_{y}(x)$. Thus $\\widetilde{x}$ changes only how this\nforce is divided between deterministic motion and bounces. By the\n$\\beta$-smoothness of $V$,\n$\\|h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)\\|=\\|\\nabla V(x)-\\nabla V(\\widetilde{x})\\|\\leq\\beta\\,\\|x-\\widetilde{x}\\|\\,.$\nDrawing the reference point from the same localized conditional law keeps\nthe residual gradient small and reduces the expected number of bounces per\nrun of the half-turn process to order $\\beta\\eta\\sqrt{d}$; see\nProposition 3.3.\nHaving specified the dynamics, it remains to choose the integration\nhorizon. For fixed $(y,\\widetilde{x})$, the deterministic part of\n(3.5) is harmonic motion about its equilibrium\nposition $c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}$. In the absence of bounces, its position at\ntime $t$ satisfies\n$x_{t}-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}=(x_{0}-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})\\cos t+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p_{0}\\sin t\\,.$\n(3.6)\nAs discussed earlier, we choose the integration horizon $t=\\pi$, corresponding to one\nhalf-period. This is the first non-zero harmonic time at which the\nposition $x_{\\pi}$ no longer depends on the initial momentum $p_{0}$.\nWithout bounces, the resulting map is\n$(x_{\\pi},p_{\\pi})=(2c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}-x_{0},-p_{0})\\,.$\n(3.7)\nThus, the position is reflected through the equilibrium position\n$c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ and retains its dependence on $x_{0}$. Bounce\nevents correct for the residual force along the trajectory, while the\nfixed horizon $t=\\pi$ retains the memory-preserving structure of the\nhalf-turn process.\nThe construction is now complete. The following algorithm gives its\npathwise prescription: it draws the reference point and momentum, evolves\nthe process with generator (3.5) for time $\\pi$, and\nreturns only the final position.\nAlgorithm 1 Simulation of the ideal conditional half-turn process\n1:\nInput: state $(x,y)$\n2:\nDraw $\\widetilde{X}\\sim\\pi_{\\eta}^{X\\mid Y=y}$ and\n$p_{0}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ independently.\n3:\nQuery $\\nabla V(\\widetilde{X})$.\n4:\nSet $x_{0}\\leftarrow x$.\n5:\nEvolve $(x_{t},p_{t})$ according to\n$\\dot{x}=\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\dot{p}=-\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{\\eta}}-\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,\\nabla V(\\widetilde{X})\\,,$\n(3.8)\nuntil $t=\\pi$.\n6:\nDuring the evolution, generate bounce events at rate\n$\\lambda_{\\widetilde{X}}(x,p)=\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,\\bigl[p^{\\top}[\\nabla V(x)-\\nabla V(\\widetilde{X})]\\bigr]_{+}\\,.$\n(3.9)\n7:\nAt each bounce, set\n$p_{t}\\leftarrow R_{\\nabla V(x_{t})-\\nabla V(\\widetilde{X})}p_{t}$.\n8:\nreturn $x_{\\pi}$\nProposition 3.1 (Well-posedness and stationarity of the conditional phase space process).\nFor every fixed $y,\\widetilde{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and initial phase space point\n$(x_{0},p_{0})$, the event-driven dynamics in Algorithm 1, with\nthe reference point fixed at $\\widetilde{x}$, defines a unique\nnon-explosive, time-homogeneous Markov process. The phase space measure\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}$ is stationary for this process. In particular, the\nreturned position $x_{\\pi}$ is well-defined almost surely.\nThe well-posedness and stationarity assertions are proved in\nSection A.1.\n\nREGION appendixA1-construction [533213, 575646]\nA.1 The fixed-reference phase space process\nProof of the well-posedness assertion in\nProposition 3.1.\nFix $y,\\widetilde{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$. In the notation of\n(3.4), the bounce rate\n(3.9) reads\n$\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)=\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,[p^{\\top}h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\\,.$\nThe deterministic harmonic flow, namely the solution\n(4.5) of (3.8), and\nthe bounce map are\n$\\displaystyle\\Phi_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)$\n$\\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\bigl(c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}+(x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})\\cos t+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p\\sin t,\\;-\\eta^{-1/2}(x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})\\sin t+p\\cos t\\bigr)\\,,$\n$\\displaystyle\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)$\n$\\displaystyle\\coloneqq(x,R_{h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptR_{0}\\coloneqq I\\,.$\nThe bounce map is an involution. Although there is a possible discontinuity at $h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)=0$, this creates no ambiguity\nbecause the event rate vanishes there.\nLet $(E_{n})_{n\\geq 1}$ be independent $\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$\nrandom variables. Starting from\n$\\zeta_{0}\\coloneqq(x_{0},p_{0})$ at time $T_{0}\\coloneqq 0$, define recursively\n$\\displaystyle S_{n+1}$\n$\\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\inf\\Bigl\\{u\\geq 0:\\int_{0}^{u}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\Phi_{s}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}}))\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\geq E_{n+1}\\Bigr\\}\\,,$\n(A.1)\n$\\displaystyle T_{n+1}$\n$\\displaystyle\\coloneqq T_{n}+S_{n+1}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\zeta_{T_{n+1}}\\coloneqq\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}\\bigl(\\Phi_{S_{n+1}}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}})\\bigr)\\,,$\n(A.2)\nwhen $S_{n+1}<\\infty$. If the set is empty, set\n$T_{n+1}=S_{n+1}=\\infty$ and stop the recursion. Between jumps, set\n$\\zeta_{T_{n}+t}=\\Phi_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}})$ for\n$0\\leq t<S_{n+1}$.\nTo prove non-explosion, define the shifted harmonic energy\n$H_{y,\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\\coloneqq\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\bigl\\{\\eta^{-1}\\,\\|x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\|^{2}+\\|p\\|^{2}\\bigr\\}\\,.$\nThe flow and each bounce preserve this energy. Hence, with\n$\\mathcal{E}\\coloneqq H_{y,\\widetilde{x}}(x_{0},p_{0})$, every state produced by\nthe recursion satisfies\n$\\|p\\|\\leq\\sqrt{2\\mathcal{E}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\|\\leq\\sqrt{2\\eta\\mathcal{E}}\\,.$\nBecause $\\nabla V$ is $\\beta$-Lipschitz,\n$\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\\leq\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})$ throughout the path, where\n$\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})\\coloneqq\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,\\beta\\sqrt{2\\mathcal{E}}\\,\\bigl\\{\\sqrt{2\\eta\\mathcal{E}}+\\|c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}-\\widetilde{x}\\|\\bigr\\}\\,.$\nHence,\n$\\int_{0}^{u}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\Phi_{s}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}}))\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\leq\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})\\,u\\,.$\nIf $\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})=0$, the event rate\nvanishes and there are no jumps. Otherwise,\n(A.1) implies, for every finite waiting time,\n$S_{n+1}\\geq\\frac{E_{n+1}}{\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})}\\,.$\nIf the recursion does not terminate, it follows that\n$T_{n}\\geq\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{n}E_{j}}{\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})}\\longrightarrow\\infty$\nalmost surely by the strong law of large numbers. Thus, the jump times\ncannot accumulate, and the recursive construction defines a unique\nprocess for all $t\\geq 0$. Finally, the\nmemoryless property of the exponential clocks gives the time-homogeneous\nMarkov property. This is precisely the integrated hazard construction\nof Davis [12, Section 2].\n∎\nWith the process now constructed, we prove the stationarity assertion in\nProposition 3.1 and record the stronger operator facts used\nto analyze the averaged kernel. Recall $\\nu_{\\eta,y}$ from\n(3.1), and define momentum\nreversal by\n$\\mathsf{R}(x,p)\\coloneqq(x,-p)\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt(\\mathsf{R}F)(x,p)\\coloneqq F(\\mathsf{R}(x,p))\\,.$\nAs usual, we use the same symbol for $\\nu_{\\eta,y}$ and its Lebesgue\ndensity:\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}(x,p)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V_{y}(x)-\\frac{\\|p\\|^{2}}{2}\\Bigr\\}\\,.$\nProposition A.1 (Path reversal, invariance, and the $L^{2}$ semigroup).\nLet $(\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}})_{t\\geq 0}$ be the Markov transition\noperators of the process constructed in\nProposition 3.1. For every $t\\geq 0$,\n$(\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}})^{*}=\\mathsf{R}\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\mathsf{R}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{on }L^{2}(\\nu_{\\eta,y})\\,.$\n(A.3)\nConsequently:\n(i)\n$\\nu_{\\eta,y}$ is invariant;\n(ii)\n$(\\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}})_{t\\geq 0}$ is a strongly continuous\ncontraction semigroup on $L^{2}(\\nu_{\\eta,y})$;\n(iii)\n\nREGION davis-reference [1445364, 1448631]\nNote: Available online at https://chewisinho.github.io/\nCited by: §1,\nfootnote 1.\n[DAV84]\nM. H. A. Davis (1984)\nPiecewise-deterministic Markov processes: a general class of non-diffusion stochastic models.\nJournal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series B 46 (3), pp. 353–388.\nExternal Links: Document\nCited by: §A.1,\nAppendix A.\n[DB23]\nP. Dobson and J. Bierkens (2023)\n\nREGION S1.p1.1 [47160, 49051]\nWe study the problem of sampling from the probability measure\n$\\mu(\\mathrm{d}x)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ on $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, where\n$V\\in C^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{d})$ satisfies, for $0<\\alpha\\leq\\beta$,\n\nREGION S1.E1 [49052, 50640]\n$\\alpha I\\preceq\\nabla^{2}V(x)\\preceq\\beta I\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\kappa\\coloneqq\\tfrac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\,.$\n(1.1)\n\nREGION S2.SS2.p1.1 [95559, 96221]\nOur algorithm operates on the augmented distribution underlying the\nproximal sampler of Lee, Shen, and Tian [17]. For a\nscale $\\eta\\in(0,1/\\beta]$, consider the joint law\n",
